Replies interleave answers beneath the quoted lines, so a question or
request for the reader can sit anywhere in a long mail and be missed.
Pat then has to read the whole reply to learn whether the work is done
and what he must do next.

A fixed summary at the end answers that in one place. It also makes a
stalled task visible: work is either done or blocked on something only
the reader can give, so a reply that is not done and asks for nothing
shows that the work stopped without reason.
